43:Human Connectome Project Young Adult Study S900 Release (HCP YA)
45:All subjects belong to a sibship, were defined as controls (n=726; 328 male, 398 female), and considered to be broadly healthy without a history of neurodevelopmental, psychiatric, or neurological disorders (Van Essen et al., 2012).
47:The Lifespan Human Connectome Project in Aging (HCP AGE)
49:All subjects were defined as controls (n=701; 306 male, 395 female) and had typical aging without major psychiatric or neurologic disorders. Subjects were screened for cognitive deficits and while dementia like Alzheimer's disease was excluded, mild to moderate cognitive deficits were allowed depending on the age of the subject (Bookheimer et al., 2019).
51:The Human Connectome Project for Early Psychosis (HCP EP)
53:All subjects were assessed with the SCID-5-RV. Control subjects (n=56; 37 male, 19 female) did not have formal psychiatric diagnoses or history of psychiatric hospitalization. Subjects with non-affective psychosis (Early SCZ) (n=83; 59 male, 24 female) met DSM V criteria for schizophrenia, schizophreniform, schizoaffective, psychosis NOS, delusional disorder, or brief psychotic disorder with onset within five years of study enrollment. Subjects from the HCP EP cohort with affective psychosis (i.e. bipolar disorder with psychotic features or depression with psychotic freatures) were not included in the current analyses. (Lewandowski et al., 2020).
